{
  "slug": "artist:dune",
  "name": "Dune",
  "editorial_article": "Founded in 1995 in Münster, Germany, by Oliver Froning, Jens Oettrich, and Bernd Burhoff, the electronic act Dune emerged as a significant force in the mid-90s happy hardcore scene. The trio named the project after the 1984 film adaptation of the Frank Herbert novel. Their debut single, \"Hardcore Vibes,\" featured spoken word vocals by Froning's niece, while the third single, \"Can't Stop Raving,\" utilized heavily pitched-up vocals from singer Tina Lagao. In 1996, dancer Verena von Strenge joined as the group's primary vocalist, featuring on the album \"Expedicion,\" which included the Star Trek-inspired single \"Million Miles from Home.\" By 1997, the band partnered with the London Session Orchestra to record a cover of Queen's \"Who Wants to Live Forever,\" reaching the second position on the German charts. Internal friction later led to the departure of von Strenge and the eventual cancellation of the 1999 \"Reunion\" album following legal action from A7 regarding the single \"Heaven.\"",
